.TH PEEKIN-CONFIG 5 2026-09-25 "PeekIn 1.0.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
peekin-config \- face authentication settings, /etc/peekin/config.ini
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I /etc/peekin/config.ini
holds the system-wide settings of PeekIn, in INI format:
.RB [ section ]
headers and
.IB key " = " value
lines; lines starting with
.B #
are comments.
Change it with
.BR peekin-settings (1),
.BI "peekin set " "key value"
or
.BR "peekin config" .
Booleans are
.BR true / false
(also yes/no, on/off, 1/0).
.PP
An invalid value of a security setting (marked
.B security
below) turns face authentication off until it is fixed; the password is used
instead.
.BR "peekin doctor"
reports every invalid value.
.SH [core]
.TP
.BR disabled " = false"
.B security.
Turn face authentication off everywhere.
The
.BR peekin (1)
command still works.
.TP
.BR movement_challenge " = true"
.B security.
After recognising the face, ask for a random sequence of three head turns.
Off: unlock by looking at the camera, which is faster but lets a photo of the
user work more easily.
.TP
.BR require_movement_without_ir " = true"
.B security.
With
.B movement_challenge
off, still ask for the head turns unless the camera in use is an
administrator-approved infrared camera (see
.BR ir_only ).
A photo on paper or a phone then cannot unlock with a colour camera.
.TP
.BR max_failed_attempts " = 5"
.B security.
After this many rejected faces in a row (face not recognised, or head
movement not followed) face login pauses for 15 minutes or until the user
logs in with the password.
0 turns the limit off.
.TP
.BR detection_notice " = true"
Show the face-check message before scanning.
.TP
.BR no_confirmation " = false"
Do not show \(lqFace recognised: \&...\(rq after a match.
.TP
.BR abort_if_ssh " = true"
.B security.
Never scan in an SSH session.
.TP
.BR abort_if_lid_closed " = true"
.B security.
Never scan while the laptop lid is closed.
.TP
.BR workaround " = off"
Only
.B off
is supported: the scan runs first and the password prompt follows if it fails.
.SH [services]
Where face authentication may be used.
All are
.BR security .
.TP
.BR sudo " = true"
sudo and sudo \-i in a terminal.
.TP
.BR login_screen " = true"
The GDM login screen after starting the computer.
The keyring stays locked until the password is entered.
.TP
.BR lock_screen " = true"
Unlocking a running GNOME session.
.TP
.BR polkit " = false"
Password dialogs of applications and pkexec (experimental).
.TP
.BR console " = false"
Logging in on a local text console (experimental).
.SH [video]
.TP
.BR device_path " = none"
The camera.
.B none
chooses one each time: an administrator-approved infrared camera first, then
a colour camera.
Otherwise a device path such as
.IR /dev/v4l/by-id/\&... .
.TP
.BR ir_only " = false"
.B security.
Accept only capture nodes attested in
.IR /etc/peekin/ir-profiles.json ,
which pins the camera's physical USB identity.
This is not liveness detection.
.TP
.BR min_similarity " = 0.363"
.B security.
How similar a live face must be to an enrolled one (cosine similarity, 0.2 to
1.0).
Higher is stricter.
Measured on LFW with a 5-sample face model, about 1 stranger in 240 matches at
0.363, 1 in 5,700 at 0.45 and 1 in 51,000 at 0.50 (see
.IR THRESHOLDS.md ).
.TP
.BR personal_threshold " = false"
.B security.
Raise
.B min_similarity
for each user from their own enrollment: the least similar pair of samples
within one face model, minus 0.30, at most 0.50.
It never lowers the threshold.
Measured error rates:
.IR /usr/share/doc/peekin/THRESHOLDS.md .
.TP
.BR timeout " = 3"
.B security.
Seconds to look for the face before the password is asked for, 1 to 10.
The head-movement check gets up to 8 more seconds.
.TP
.BR dark_threshold " = 80"
Skip a frame when more than this percentage of its pixels are in the darkest
eighth of the brightness range.
Infrared cameras often alternate lit and unlit frames.
.TP
.BR brighten_screen " = true"
In a dark room, raise the built-in screen to full brightness while a colour
camera scans, and restore it afterwards.
Not on the text console or in polkit dialogs.
.TP
.BR frame_width " = 640, " frame_height " = 480"
Capture size; \-1 uses the camera's default.
.TP
.BR max_height " = 320"
Largest image height given to the face detector; larger frames are scaled
down.
.TP
.BR force_mjpeg " = false"
Capture Motion JPEG, for cameras whose raw frames decode badly.
.TP
.BR exposure " = \-1"
Fixed exposure value, disabling automatic exposure; \-1 keeps it automatic.
.TP
.BR device_fps " = \-1"
Capture frame rate; \-1 keeps the camera's default.
Some infrared emitters only work at a particular rate.
.TP
.BR rotate " = 0"
0: landscape only; 1: landscape and portrait; 2: portrait only.
.TP
.BR warn_no_device " = true"
Log a warning when the camera is missing.
.SH [snapshots]
.TP
.BR save_failed " = false"
Keep a JPEG photo each time a face is seen but not accepted (wrong face, or the
head movement not followed), in
.IR /var/log/peekin .
Only root and the
.B peekin
service account can read them; the settings app shows each user the photos of
attempts on their own account, after administrator authentication.
Attempts without a face, camera errors and successful logins are never kept.
.TP
.BR keep " = 20"
How many photos to keep, 1 to 100; the oldest are deleted.
.SH [debug]
.TP
.BR end_report " = false"
Log a timing report for every attempt.
.SH COMPATIBILITY KEYS
These older keys are accepted so existing files still load.
.BR use_cnn ,
.BR certainty ,
.BR timeout_notice ,
.BR suppress_unknown ,
.BR verbose_stamps " and"
.B gtk_stdout
have no effect.
.B [snapshots] save_successful
is not supported and is ignored with a log message.
.B [rubberstamps] enabled
= true turns face authentication off, because rubber stamps are not
supported.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/peekin/config.ini
.br
.I /etc/peekin/ir-profiles.json
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peekin (1),
.BR peekin-settings (1),
.BR pam_peekin (8)
